half of global SMS traffic.

00:44:24.020 --> 00:44:27.250
It serves up to two
million queries per second,

00:44:27.250 --> 00:44:32.240
and consumes network bandwidth
second only to YouTube.

00:44:32.240 --> 00:44:38.110
And yet they've never
hired an operations team.

00:44:38.110 --> 00:44:42.340
And they've been able
to scale to that level.

00:44:42.340 --> 00:44:45.110
For these reasons, to support
those kind of workloads,

00:44:45.110 --> 00:44:48.490
we've built a different
kind of cloud,

00:44:48.490 --> 00:44:50.100
one that's based
on scalable data

00:44:50.100 --> 00:44:52.830
and machine-learning
services to let you much more

00:44:52.830 --> 00:44:56.200
easily create rich,
context-aware applications

00:44:56.200 --> 00:44:59.450
with automated deployment and
management capabilities that

00:44:59.450 --> 00:45:01.810
let you focus on
building great software

00:45:01.810 --> 00:45:05.706
and shipping it multiple
times an hour if you want to.

00:45:09.530 --> 00:45:12.300
To understand how this
cloud is different,

00:45:12.300 --> 00:45:15.470
it's useful to look at
where a cloud came from.

00:45:15.470 --> 00:45:19.770
Cloud really started
about 20 years ago

00:45:19.770 --> 00:45:24.690
with the co-location model where
users could run their gear,

00:45:24.690 --> 00:45:28.110
run their hardware in
someone else's building.

00:45:28.110 --> 00:45:32.090
They could use a data center
without owning a data center.

00:45:32.090 --> 00:45:33.620
This had profound implications.

00:45:33.620 --> 00:45:35.330
It was a great
financial engineering

00:45:35.330 --> 00:45:38.970
move for many companies.

00:45:38.970 --> 00:45:40.680
What most of us
think of as cloud

00:45:40.680 --> 00:45:43.860
today, these virtualized
data centers,

00:45:43.860 --> 00:45:46.170
this virtualized
computing model,

00:45:46.170 --> 00:45:51.400
shares a lot in common
with the co-location model.

00:45:51.400 --> 00:45:54.610
The basic building blocks
of computing are the same.

00:45:54.610 --> 00:46:00.460
You're still dealing with
CPUs, with storage arrays,

00:46:00.460 --> 00:46:02.110
with load balancers.

00:46:02.110 --> 00:46:03.960
But now, you're
dealing with them

00:46:03.960 --> 00:46:05.890
as software-defined
virtual devices.

00:46:09.090 --> 00:46:12.880
What was a purchase
order has become an API.

00:46:12.880 --> 00:46:16.620
And this is had profound
implications for many people.

00:46:16.620 --> 00:46:19.160
But it's still too
hard to orchestrate

00:46:19.160 --> 00:46:21.490
all those components
to work together

00:46:21.490 --> 00:46:24.720
in a dynamic
environment or at scale,

00:46:24.720 --> 00:46:26.690
even when they're virtualized.

00:46:26.690 --> 00:46:30.710
The second wave a cloud
model forces most developers

00:46:30.710 --> 00:46:34.790
to spend most of their time
doing things other than making

00:46:34.790 --> 00:46:37.800
great software.

00:46:37.800 --> 00:46:40.650
Do we have enough servers?

00:46:40.650 --> 00:46:43.250
Are they all patched?

00:46:43.250 --> 00:46:46.180
Did we buy too much capacity?

00:46:46.180 --> 00:46:48.290
Are we wasting money?

00:46:48.290 --> 00:46:51.910
In this second wave of cloud,
this virtualize center model,

00:46:51.910 --> 00:46:54.440
this never gets any easier.

00:46:54.440 --> 00:46:59.040
In fact, if you're successful,
it gets a whole lot worse,

00:46:59.040 --> 00:47:01.480
as you have to re-architect
your infrastructure

00:47:01.480 --> 00:47:05.080
as your application grows.

00:47:05.080 --> 00:47:08.360
But we will admit,
we used to run

00:47:08.360 --> 00:47:11.790
this same kind of
infrastructure inside Google

00:47:11.790 --> 00:47:14.050
until about 10 years ago.

00:47:14.050 --> 00:47:14.550
Right?

00:47:14.550 --> 00:47:17.770
And then we realized
we had to reinvent

00:47:17.770 --> 00:47:20.990
how we did software
infrastructure to keep up

00:47:20.990 --> 00:47:23.180
with our business ambitions.

00:47:23.180 --> 00:47:25.470
So we rebuilt our
infrastructure based

00:47:25.470 --> 00:47:29.000
on containers, the
technology that we invented.

00:47:29.000 --> 00:47:31.930
We provided the Linux foundation
as now part of the core Linux

00:47:31.930 --> 00:47:35.400
distribution, based on
containers rather than

00:47:35.400 --> 00:47:37.820
computers.

00:47:37.820 --> 00:47:42.590
Products like YouTube,
and Gmail, and Google Apps

00:47:42.590 --> 00:47:46.090
serve millions of
users every second.

00:47:46.090 --> 00:47:48.370
And their developers
are able to put code

00:47:48.370 --> 00:47:52.131
into production
multiple times a day.

00:47:52.131 --> 00:47:52.630
Right?

00:47:52.630 --> 00:47:55.760
And those applications
are resilient and secure.

00:47:55.760 --> 00:47:59.540
What makes this possible are
highly-automated orchestration

00:47:59.540 --> 00:48:03.760
tools that let developers deploy
signed binaries into containers

00:48:03.760 --> 00:48:06.780
without ever specifying
anything about the underlying

00:48:06.780 --> 00:48:08.840
infrastructure.

00:48:08.840 --> 00:48:14.570
Our developers never
spin up a server,

00:48:14.570 --> 00:48:19.840
never configure a network,
never patch an operating system.

00:48:19.840 --> 00:48:24.180
Instead, they focus on
building great software.

00:48:24.180 --> 00:48:26.400
We call this the
third wave a cloud.

00:48:26.400 --> 00:48:29.388
It's a combination of automated
services and scalable data.

00:48:36.329 --> 00:48:38.690
Got one more.

00:48:38.690 --> 00:48:41.670
So this is really what
some people are referring

00:48:41.670 --> 00:48:43.366
to as serverless computing.

00:48:43.366 --> 00:48:45.490
And it's something that
has been talked about quite

00:48:45.490 --> 00:48:46.970
a bit in our industry lately.

00:48:46.970 --> 00:48:50.430
But it's embedded deeply inside
Google and inside Google Cloud

00:48:50.430 --> 00:48:51.840
Platform.

00:48:51.840 --> 00:48:54.090
For the past decade, it's
how developers at Google

00:48:54.090 --> 00:48:57.520
have worked, with products
like App Engine, which

00:48:57.520 --> 00:49:00.520
lets you run a snippet of the
code, run a snippet of code

00:49:00.520 --> 00:49:04.570
in the cloud and scale it up
or down, all the way to zero,

00:49:04.570 --> 00:49:08.570
and only pay for it if
a user's accessing it;

00:49:08.570 --> 00:49:10.450
products like
Container Engine that

00:49:10.450 --> 00:49:14.120
lets you deploy code without
specifying what service it's

00:49:14.120 --> 00:49:17.820
going to run on; or
products like BigQuery,

00:49:17.820 --> 00:49:21.100
our NoOps data
warehouse appliance

00:49:21.100 --> 00:49:22.990
that's low cost
and low operational

00:49:22.990 --> 00:49:25.670
overhead allow companies
to deploy it very widely

00:49:25.670 --> 00:49:28.750
across their
organizations to push

00:49:28.750 --> 00:49:32.220
data-driven decision-making
across their company.

00:49:32.220 --> 00:49:34.060
The code just executes.

00:49:34.060 --> 00:49:37.750
And as an application developer,
you never manage servers,

00:49:37.750 --> 00:49:40.460
and you never pay for any
servers you're not actually

00:49:40.460 --> 00:49:41.560
using.

00:49:41.560 --> 00:49:43.540
Isn't that what
cloud's supposed to be?

00:49:43.540 --> 00:49:47.180
Flexible computing resources
available on demand.

00:49:47.180 --> 00:49:49.540
And at Google, we have the
most integrated approach

00:49:49.540 --> 00:49:52.640
to serverless architecture
across all of our products

00:49:52.640 --> 00:49:55.864
so that you're able to integrate
it into the way you do things

00:49:55.864 --> 00:49:57.030
without rewriting your code.

00:50:00.200 --> 00:50:04.730
But if you're like most
people, like most companies,

00:50:04.730 --> 00:50:08.310
you're also running
traditional virtual workloads.

00:50:08.310 --> 00:50:10.880
You're not just doing new
application development,

00:50:10.880 --> 00:50:12.760
new web scale computing.

00:50:12.760 --> 00:50:14.330
And part of what
you need to do is

00:50:14.330 --> 00:50:17.420
evaluate what's the right
cloud for your existing

00:50:17.420 --> 00:50:18.860
virtual workloads.

00:50:18.860 --> 00:50:22.410
It turns out that in order to
do a great job on third wave

00:50:22.410 --> 00:50:26.640
cloud, you've also got to do a
great job on second wave cloud.

00:50:26.640 --> 00:50:31.050
And so there are number
of capabilities in GCP

00:50:31.050 --> 00:50:33.280
that make it an excellent
environment for existing

00:50:33.280 --> 00:50:36.792
workloads, in almost every
case, the best environment

00:50:36.792 --> 00:50:37.750
for existing workloads.

00:50:37.750 --> 00:50:40.230
And what I want to do now
is go through a number

00:50:40.230 --> 00:50:41.880
of those features
and show you how

00:50:41.880 --> 00:50:44.350
Google Cloud Platform is
different for your existing

00:50:44.350 --> 00:50:47.080
workloads.

00:50:47.080 --> 00:50:50.260
One feature is custom
virtual machines.

00:50:50.260 --> 00:50:52.710
This lets you design
a virtual machine

00:50:52.710 --> 00:50:54.870
for your particular workload.

00:50:54.870 --> 00:50:58.380
It's so you don't
routinely over-provision.

00:50:58.380 --> 00:51:01.380
On average, this saves
our customers about 19%

00:51:01.380 --> 00:51:05.160
when you look across all the
computing happening on GCP.

00:51:05.160 --> 00:51:08.860
In some cases, it saved
customers a lot more.

00:51:08.860 --> 00:51:12.010
Let's say, for example, you want
to have a virtual machine that

00:51:12.010 --> 00:51:13.890
needs 18 cores.

00:51:13.890 --> 00:51:16.270
But if you look at our
competitor's price list,

00:51:16.270 --> 00:51:21.610
they have an offering at
16 cores and at 32 cores.

00:51:21.610 --> 00:51:26.280
In Google Cloud Platform, just
design a machine with 18 cores.

00:51:26.280 --> 00:51:29.050
Build the virtual
machine shape that you

00:51:29.050 --> 00:51:30.880
need for your workload.

00:51:30.880 --> 00:51:32.770
It's really simple.

00:51:32.770 --> 00:51:35.250
Use only what you need.

00:51:35.250 --> 00:51:37.172
Pay only for what you use.

00:51:37.172 --> 00:51:39.255
This is very different
from other cloud providers.

00:51:42.140 --> 00:51:44.090
If you're running
Hadoop today, if you're

00:51:44.090 --> 00:51:48.770
doing big data in the cloud, we
have a product called DataProc.

00:51:48.770 --> 00:51:52.300
It's a managed Hadoop
and SPARC distribution.

00:51:52.300 --> 00:51:55.610
You can spin up a
cluster in 90 seconds.

00:51:55.610 --> 00:51:56.920
Spin it down.

00:51:56.920 --> 00:51:59.040
Don't pay for it if
you're not using it.

00:51:59.040 --> 00:52:02.020
Because it's so dynamic, and
we can so quickly bring it up,

00:52:02.020 --> 00:52:04.550
you don't have to
leave it running.

00:52:04.550 --> 00:52:07.360
You can run it when you need
it, and take it down when you

00:52:07.360 --> 00:52:08.060
don't.

00:52:08.060 --> 00:52:09.840
This is unlike what
our competitors

00:52:09.840 --> 00:52:13.720
do where because it takes so
long to bring up the cluster,

00:52:13.720 --> 00:52:16.510
the customers tend to leave
it running, and paying

00:52:16.510 --> 00:52:18.050
for it while it runs.

00:52:18.050 --> 00:52:21.360
Again, use only what you need.

00:52:21.360 --> 00:52:24.870
Pay only for what you use.

00:52:24.870 --> 00:52:26.660
Archival storage
has traditionally

00:52:26.660 --> 00:52:29.385
meant trading speed for cost.

00:52:29.385 --> 00:52:30.430
Right?

00:52:30.430 --> 00:52:33.120
So you can either
get cheap storage

00:52:33.120 --> 00:52:35.310
and wait for hours to
get your data back,

00:52:35.310 --> 00:52:38.700
or you can pay for
performance storage

00:52:38.700 --> 00:52:41.460
and have access to your
data in milliseconds.

00:52:41.460 --> 00:52:44.500
What we've done is combine
these two with an offering

00:52:44.500 --> 00:52:49.450
called Nearline that gives
you millisecond access to data

00:52:49.450 --> 00:52:53.340
for pennies a gigabyte,
combining the speed of disk

00:52:53.340 --> 00:52:54.420
with the price of tape.

00:52:56.990 --> 00:53:00.890
VM startup times, VM startup
times on Google Cloud Platform

00:53:00.890 --> 00:53:05.240
are just about 30 seconds now.

00:53:05.240 --> 00:53:10.150
We continue to improve
performance as we go along.

00:53:10.150 --> 00:53:13.970
This means that on
Google Cloud Platform,

00:53:13.970 --> 00:53:17.150
you can start up
100 virtual machines

00:53:17.150 --> 00:53:20.170
in the time it takes to
start one virtual machine

00:53:20.170 --> 00:53:22.340
on our competitor's cloud.

00:53:22.340 --> 00:53:23.480
It's 100 to 1.

00:53:23.480 --> 00:53:25.677
That's two orders of magnitude.

00:53:25.677 --> 00:53:27.260
So I just want to
give you an example,

00:53:27.260 --> 00:53:29.690
so you can really
understand this difference.

00:53:29.690 --> 00:53:32.935
Today, I went for a
run before this event.

00:53:32.935 --> 00:53:35.610
I was running around
Roppongi Hills.

00:53:35.610 --> 00:53:38.920
I was running about six miles
an hour, so I run pretty slow.

00:53:42.000 --> 00:53:44.900
When I fly back
to San Francisco,

00:53:44.900 --> 00:53:49.170
the plane that I fly on will
go almost 600 miles an hour.

00:53:49.170 --> 00:53:51.470
That's two orders of
magnitude difference.

00:53:51.470 --> 00:53:54.840
The difference between
a jog and a jet plane,

00:53:54.840 --> 00:53:57.382
that's the difference
between Google Cloud

00:53:57.382 --> 00:53:58.590
Platform and our competitors.

00:54:02.090 --> 00:54:05.520
High-performance virtual
machine networking,

00:54:05.520 --> 00:54:10.450
we deliver 16 gigabits
per second to almost all

00:54:10.450 --> 00:54:12.450
the virtual machines
in our rate card.

00:54:12.450 --> 00:54:17.530
Only this very smallest, the
very entry level offering,

00:54:17.530 --> 00:54:23.380
has a lower bandwidth
network provisioning

00:54:23.380 --> 00:54:24.650
to that virtual machine.

00:54:24.650 --> 00:54:26.390
What this means is
your virtual machines

00:54:26.390 --> 00:54:28.210
are never starved for data.

00:54:28.210 --> 00:54:32.490
And you don't have to provision
the most expensive machine

00:54:32.490 --> 00:54:36.331
types in our rate card in order
to do data-intensive tasks.

00:54:36.331 --> 00:54:36.830
Right?

00:54:36.830 --> 00:54:40.196
For Google Cloud, we believe
that all your virtual machines

00:54:40.196 --> 00:54:41.820
should have access
to all the data they

00:54:41.820 --> 00:54:43.500
need to do their work.

00:54:43.500 --> 00:54:45.510
No other cloud offers
such high performance

00:54:45.510 --> 00:54:47.080
across all these instance types.

00:54:47.080 --> 00:54:49.455
And on GCP, it's a standard
feature at no extra cost.

00:54:52.310 --> 00:54:55.200
As Rick said, we
operate a global network

00:54:55.200 --> 00:54:59.486
with 77 points of
presence across the world.

00:54:59.486 --> 00:55:01.360
This means that you can
peer with our network

00:55:01.360 --> 00:55:04.580
and get access to your
customers worldwide.

00:55:04.580 --> 00:55:08.900
You can see we have a number of
points of presence around Asia.

00:55:08.900 --> 00:55:10.550
What's more, our
networking functions

00:55:10.550 --> 00:55:13.460
are delivered as
managed services.

00:55:13.460 --> 00:55:15.770
They're not hardware
virtual appliances

00:55:15.770 --> 00:55:19.540
that you have to configure, just
managed services that you call.

00:55:19.540 --> 00:55:22.850
A great example of this is
our global load balancer.

00:55:22.850 --> 00:55:23.350
Right?

00:55:23.350 --> 00:55:26.240
Instead of having essentially
virtual appliances

00:55:26.240 --> 00:55:27.990
that you have to
configure and synchronize

00:55:27.990 --> 00:55:32.400
DNS settings across
availability zones,

00:55:32.400 --> 00:55:34.950
our global load balancer is
just a service that you call.

00:55:34.950 --> 00:55:37.240
It's one URL for
your application,

00:55:37.240 --> 00:55:40.040
and we load balance across
the regions you specify.

00:55:40.040 --> 00:55:43.410
Now, what's more,
it scales from 0

00:55:43.410 --> 00:55:47.680
to 1 million queries per
second in less than a second.

00:55:47.680 --> 00:55:49.940
You don't have to
call us up beforehand

00:55:49.940 --> 00:55:52.880
and let us know that you're
about to have some load.

00:55:52.880 --> 00:55:54.460
The service just
responds to the load

00:55:54.460 --> 00:55:57.450
as it comes in, very,
very different from what

00:55:57.450 --> 00:56:00.820
our competitors offer.

00:56:00.820 --> 00:56:05.140
So pricing, pricing is still
very, very important for cloud,

00:56:05.140 --> 00:56:07.665
particularly if you're
running large scale workloads,

00:56:07.665 --> 00:56:11.150
or you're making a large
commitment to cloud computing.

00:56:11.150 --> 00:56:13.890
The Google Cloud
Platform, we offer,

00:56:13.890 --> 00:56:17.150
in total, a discount
of up to 59%,

00:56:17.150 --> 00:56:18.750
relative to our competitors.

00:56:18.750 --> 00:56:20.630
I just want to break
that down for you.

00:56:20.630 --> 00:56:23.210
The custom virtual
machines that I

00:56:23.210 --> 00:56:28.320
talked about a few minutes ago,
that's 19% savings right there.

00:56:28.320 --> 00:56:31.780
And then our rate card, our
standard list price rate card,

00:56:31.780 --> 00:56:33.710
we're significantly
cheaper than competitors

00:56:33.710 --> 00:56:35.745
because the scale
we're operating at.

00:56:35.745 --> 00:56:37.870
We have purchasing power,
and we have efficiencies,

00:56:37.870 --> 00:56:40.224
just because we're twice
as big as they are.

00:56:40.224 --> 00:56:41.640
And then there's
a set of features

00:56:41.640 --> 00:56:44.740
we call customer-friendly
pricing where we've

00:56:44.740 --> 00:56:49.580
adapted our business models to
match the way customers consume

00:56:49.580 --> 00:56:51.450
cloud computing.

00:56:51.450 --> 00:56:54.840
For example, we're the
only cloud provider

00:56:54.840 --> 00:56:57.190
that offers per-minute billing.

00:56:57.190 --> 00:57:00.580
Everyone else
bills you per hour.

00:57:00.580 --> 00:57:02.490
We're also the
only cloud provider

00:57:02.490 --> 00:57:04.340
that offers a
sustained use discount.

00:57:04.340 --> 00:57:06.940
Sustained use discounts
means you automatically

00:57:06.940 --> 00:57:09.900
get a discount the more
you use the platform.

00:57:09.900 --> 00:57:14.370
We also offer preemptible
virtual machines, which

00:57:14.370 --> 00:57:16.450
are ideal for bursty workloads.

00:57:16.450 --> 00:57:19.270
Those offer a 70%
discount if we can

00:57:19.270 --> 00:57:23.590
interrupt the virtual
machine when we need it.

00:57:23.590 --> 00:57:26.950
So what's really important about
all these discounts is they

00:57:26.950 --> 00:57:28.990
all work together automatically.

00:57:31.880 --> 00:57:35.170
You don't have to do
some contract with us.

00:57:35.170 --> 00:57:36.910
You don't have to do
some fancy scripting

00:57:36.910 --> 00:57:40.140
to use different
aspects of our platform.

00:57:40.140 --> 00:57:44.190
The price savings
happen automatically.

00:57:44.190 --> 00:57:45.930
And this means that
we're just much, much

00:57:45.930 --> 00:57:48.970
easier to do business with.

00:57:48.970 --> 00:57:52.420
A company called Quizlet wrote
a comparison of Amazon Web

00:57:52.420 --> 00:57:55.070
Services and Google Cloud
Platform last March,

00:57:55.070 --> 00:57:58.010
and it's very detailed
across a number of subjects.

00:57:58.010 --> 00:58:01.010
But one of the things they
talk about is pricing.

00:58:01.010 --> 00:58:04.560
Google Cloud Platform
pricing is much friendlier,

00:58:04.560 --> 00:58:06.580
and you're likely to
save a lot of money

00:58:06.580 --> 00:58:08.480
with Google Cloud Platform.

00:58:08.480 --> 00:58:09.414
So how does this work?

00:58:12.335 --> 00:58:14.460
How can you get started
with Google Cloud Platform?

00:58:14.460 --> 00:58:16.070
How is it really different?

00:58:16.070 --> 00:58:17.550
To tell you more
about this, I am

00:58:17.550 --> 00:58:23.350
delighted to be able to
introduce my friend, my sensei,

00:58:23.350 --> 00:58:25.571
Miles Ward, the Head of--

