00:59:08.180 --> 00:59:09.020
My name's Miles.

00:59:09.020 --> 00:59:11.390
And what I'm going to do
today is to try and show you

00:59:11.390 --> 00:59:16.790
how Google has built all of
these differentiated features.

00:59:16.790 --> 00:59:19.570
And I'm going to demonstrate
three different ways

00:59:19.570 --> 00:59:22.160
that you can use
those tools to make

00:59:22.160 --> 00:59:25.630
a big impact in your business.

00:59:25.630 --> 00:59:28.260
If you look at the
constellation of services,

00:59:28.260 --> 00:59:30.410
all the different
pieces that we've

00:59:30.410 --> 00:59:34.610
built, from tools of every
different size and shape,

00:59:34.610 --> 00:59:36.630
it can look very
complicated when you

00:59:36.630 --> 00:59:38.130
look at Google Cloud Platform.

00:59:38.130 --> 00:59:40.730
We have a lot to offer.

00:59:40.730 --> 00:59:43.126
But in reality, if
you break this down

00:59:43.126 --> 00:59:44.750
into something that's
a little simpler,

00:59:44.750 --> 00:59:49.920
we've built three basic tiers of
service, first, infrastructure

00:59:49.920 --> 00:59:53.110
and operations tools that
allow you to have access

00:59:53.110 --> 00:59:57.250
to the same basic resources
that Google uses to build

00:59:57.250 --> 00:59:59.410
its incredible services.

00:59:59.410 --> 01:00:02.030
Above that, if you're
building new applications,

01:00:02.030 --> 01:00:04.970
or hosting applications
where you control the code,

01:00:04.970 --> 01:00:08.070
our application deployment
and development environment

01:00:08.070 --> 01:00:12.830
is, bar none, the easiest,
simplest, fastest way

01:00:12.830 --> 01:00:16.120
to get your code into
the hands of customers.

01:00:16.120 --> 01:00:18.000
And our data environment,
how many folks

01:00:18.000 --> 01:00:19.790
have heard of Google's
mission statement

01:00:19.790 --> 01:00:23.130
where we organize and make
useful the world's information?

01:00:23.130 --> 01:00:26.610
It turns out that's something
we have a lot of tools for.

01:00:26.610 --> 01:00:29.700
And we're really excited to
share those tools with you

01:00:29.700 --> 01:00:31.560
so that you can
organize and make

01:00:31.560 --> 01:00:34.980
useful the data that's
critical to your businesses

01:00:34.980 --> 01:00:37.290
and your customers.

01:00:37.290 --> 01:00:41.120
So if you think about each
of these different tools,

01:00:41.120 --> 01:00:43.570
they all stand on
top of each other.

01:00:43.570 --> 01:00:46.210
And the innovations that
we've made all the way down

01:00:46.210 --> 01:00:50.110
at the pavement, at the
basic foundational components

01:00:50.110 --> 01:00:53.330
of our infrastructure,
have a huge positive effect

01:00:53.330 --> 01:00:56.130
on every one of the
layers further and further

01:00:56.130 --> 01:00:58.450
up the stack.

01:00:58.450 --> 01:01:02.190
That kind of investment in
innovation over the last 17

01:01:02.190 --> 01:01:05.260
years of our business has meant
that our data centers are just

01:01:05.260 --> 01:01:06.810
a little weird.

01:01:06.810 --> 01:01:09.240
They're very different
than other environments.

01:01:09.240 --> 01:01:11.300
And I want to explain
how that works.

01:01:11.300 --> 01:01:14.030
It really starts with power.

01:01:14.030 --> 01:01:18.250
In the average data center,
about 40% of the power consumed

01:01:18.250 --> 01:01:22.690
is used for something other
than actually computing.

01:01:22.690 --> 01:01:27.220
It's used for cooling,
or power distribution,

01:01:27.220 --> 01:01:29.830
or its losses in transformation.

01:01:29.830 --> 01:01:33.210
It's because these systems are
designed in older generation

01:01:33.210 --> 01:01:34.630
technologies.

01:01:34.630 --> 01:01:36.880
Google's data centers are
some of the most efficient

01:01:36.880 --> 01:01:37.690
in the world.

01:01:37.690 --> 01:01:40.950
We use less than 10% of
our power for something

01:01:40.950 --> 01:01:42.140
other than computing.

01:01:42.140 --> 01:01:46.470
And overall, we use
less power as a total.

01:01:46.470 --> 01:01:52.000
That means we get almost 200%
more processing power out

01:01:52.000 --> 01:01:56.610
of the same electricity than
we did just five years ago.

01:01:56.610 --> 01:01:59.530
Now, when we use less
power, we spend less money.

01:01:59.530 --> 01:02:04.030
And that means our systems cost
less, which is good for you.

01:02:04.030 --> 01:02:06.300
But it also is
good for the world.

01:02:06.300 --> 01:02:08.320
Google is taking very
seriously our commitment

01:02:08.320 --> 01:02:09.520
to the environment.

01:02:09.520 --> 01:02:12.740
We're the number one
buyer in the United States

01:02:12.740 --> 01:02:15.990
of renewal energy, number
one in green energy.

01:02:15.990 --> 01:02:20.140
We're buying 2 gigawatts
of energy a year.

01:02:20.140 --> 01:02:21.800
For those who are
counting, that's

01:02:21.800 --> 01:02:25.140
roughly 2 time-traveling
DeLoreans,

01:02:25.140 --> 01:02:29.510
which is just a weird
combination for us to measure.

01:02:29.510 --> 01:02:32.260
We've been carbon
neutral since 2007.

01:02:32.260 --> 01:02:36.300
And 100% of our power
usage is carbon offset.

01:02:36.300 --> 01:02:39.680
We consider this a core
differentiation of the way

01:02:39.680 --> 01:02:43.640
that our platforms are
a steward of the earth.

01:02:43.640 --> 01:02:47.050
When you run on GCP, you
inherit that benefit.

01:02:47.050 --> 01:02:49.300
You don't pay for
excessive overhead.

01:02:49.300 --> 01:02:53.770
You don't pay for the
inefficient use of power.

01:02:53.770 --> 01:02:57.410
And we think that kind
of efficient application

01:02:57.410 --> 01:03:00.870
of infrastructure across all
of the layers of our stack

01:03:00.870 --> 01:03:05.630
make your systems better,
make your systems faster.

01:03:05.630 --> 01:03:07.976
It's not just about
building great data centers

01:03:07.976 --> 01:03:09.225
from an efficiency standpoint.

01:03:09.225 --> 01:03:12.950
It doesn't matter if they're
cheap if they aren't secure.

01:03:12.950 --> 01:03:16.650
And we've focused an
embedded security technology

01:03:16.650 --> 01:03:18.780
at the very foundation
of our businesses

01:03:18.780 --> 01:03:20.445
because security
is core to Google,

01:03:20.445 --> 01:03:23.770
and GCP is a part of Google.

01:03:23.770 --> 01:03:26.960
We've invested at every layer
of our infrastructure stack.

01:03:26.960 --> 01:03:30.230
We have differentiations
at the physical layout.

01:03:30.230 --> 01:03:32.020
We have our own kinds
of security there.

01:03:32.020 --> 01:03:34.120
We have our own networking.

01:03:34.120 --> 01:03:35.800
We have our own
computer designs.

01:03:35.800 --> 01:03:37.344
We have our own
facility designs.

01:03:37.344 --> 01:03:38.760
We have our own
software controls.

01:03:38.760 --> 01:03:40.900
We have our own
software deployment.

01:03:40.900 --> 01:03:45.190
Each of these different layers
is secured by Google engineers

01:03:45.190 --> 01:03:48.990
to ensure that your systems
are safe, that your customers'

01:03:48.990 --> 01:03:50.870
data is safe.

01:03:50.870 --> 01:03:55.100
That's done through automation,
through software, not

01:03:55.100 --> 01:03:57.040
just about applying
enough people

01:03:57.040 --> 01:03:59.010
to double check every
line and make sure

01:03:59.010 --> 01:04:02.780
that things are working, but
to ensure that automatically,

01:04:02.780 --> 01:04:05.590
your infrastructure
is protected.

01:04:05.590 --> 01:04:10.750
If it's not automatic, it's
a bug, and we have to fix it.

01:04:10.750 --> 01:04:12.929
So we've made that
investment over time,

01:04:12.929 --> 01:04:14.720
and we'll continue to
grow that investment.

01:04:14.720 --> 01:04:18.610
Some of that has paid
dividends in new inventions.

01:04:18.610 --> 01:04:21.790
We're the ones who invented
elliptic curve cryptography.

01:04:21.790 --> 01:04:23.840
We've added second-factor
authentication

01:04:23.840 --> 01:04:26.550
to all of our services, so
you can use a hardware key

01:04:26.550 --> 01:04:28.470
to ensure that you
are the only one who

01:04:28.470 --> 01:04:30.620
will have access to your tools.

01:04:30.620 --> 01:04:32.960
And all of those
innovations, and many more,

01:04:32.960 --> 01:04:37.330
including encryption in
transit and at rest for all GCP

01:04:37.330 --> 01:04:40.600
services, are built
by a team, not

01:04:40.600 --> 01:04:47.330
of 500 security guards, or 500
security auditors, or analysts,

01:04:47.330 --> 01:04:50.130
but 500 security engineers,
software developers

01:04:50.130 --> 01:04:54.700
building the cutting edge of
security technology today.

01:04:54.700 --> 01:04:57.200
These are the engineers
who discovered Heartbleed,

01:04:57.200 --> 01:04:58.710
who discovered Rowhammer.

01:04:58.710 --> 01:05:01.380
And recognize, when they find
it in our infrastructure,

01:05:01.380 --> 01:05:06.250
we fix it here first and
then release that innovation

01:05:06.250 --> 01:05:07.750
and technology to the wild.

01:05:07.750 --> 01:05:10.950
So your systems can take
advantage of that cutting edge

01:05:10.950 --> 01:05:14.320
security by being built
on Google Cloud Platform.

01:05:14.320 --> 01:05:18.075
And we do all of that oriented,
as our whole businesses

01:05:18.075 --> 01:05:19.160
is, around speed.

01:05:19.160 --> 01:05:21.630
We're the ones who said
performance is a feature.

01:05:21.630 --> 01:05:24.490
And so we're not willing
to compromise speed

01:05:24.490 --> 01:05:26.080
and performance for security.

01:05:26.080 --> 01:05:29.500
We think you can
get both together.

01:05:29.500 --> 01:05:32.670
It's not, also, about
actually being secure.

01:05:32.670 --> 01:05:35.430
We need to prove that
we're secure to others.

01:05:35.430 --> 01:05:38.940
We need to earn your trust
by earning the certifications

01:05:38.940 --> 01:05:41.980
and credentials that show
the world that we absolutely

01:05:41.980 --> 01:05:43.610
know what we're doing
around security.

01:05:43.610 --> 01:05:45.470
So we're PCI compliant.

01:05:45.470 --> 01:05:47.480
You can store credit
card data with us.

01:05:47.480 --> 01:05:48.520
We're HIPAA compliant.

01:05:48.520 --> 01:05:50.890
You can build health
care systems on us.

01:05:50.890 --> 01:05:52.800
We follow the full ISO 27000.

01:05:52.800 --> 01:05:54.580
In fact, many of
these standards,

01:05:54.580 --> 01:05:57.520
we're on the defining body for.

01:05:57.520 --> 01:05:59.510
We help the industry
as it writes

01:05:59.510 --> 01:06:02.780
new standards to
establish the security

01:06:02.780 --> 01:06:06.350
credentials that all customers
and all public providers

01:06:06.350 --> 01:06:08.070
adhere to.

01:06:08.070 --> 01:06:10.910
And it's that
constant investment

01:06:10.910 --> 01:06:13.730
in a never-ending
effort to secure

01:06:13.730 --> 01:06:18.641
our systems that differentiates
GCP from a security standpoint.

01:06:18.641 --> 01:06:20.390
But you don't have to
take my word for it.

01:06:20.390 --> 01:06:21.470
You can actually see one.

01:06:21.470 --> 01:06:23.660
Who wants to see a data center?

01:06:23.660 --> 01:06:25.170
They are awesome.

01:06:25.170 --> 01:06:26.720
They are really
cool looking inside.

01:06:26.720 --> 01:06:27.810
I promise.

01:06:27.810 --> 01:06:29.840
If you go into our
playground, you

01:06:29.840 --> 01:06:33.600
can use Google Cardboard
to take a 360 degree

01:06:33.600 --> 01:06:35.650
tour of the data center.

01:06:35.650 --> 01:06:38.120
You can see our racks,
and our switches,

01:06:38.120 --> 01:06:40.290
and our networking, and
our power distribution.

01:06:40.290 --> 01:06:41.690
It's really incredible stuff.

01:06:41.690 --> 01:06:44.520
But you'll notice-- look
around-- there will always

01:06:44.520 --> 01:06:49.460
be a security guard in view
because we take security, even

01:06:49.460 --> 01:06:53.350
in our demos, very,
very seriously.

01:06:53.350 --> 01:06:56.380
So security is important.

01:06:56.380 --> 01:06:58.420
We think that's sort
of a critical step.

01:06:58.420 --> 01:07:02.500
But we also know that what
you need from your systems

01:07:02.500 --> 01:07:05.862
is security, and by
way of availability.

01:07:05.862 --> 01:07:08.320
You need to feel secure that
they're going to keep running.

01:07:08.320 --> 01:07:10.650
And we've done a lot of
innovation in this area.

01:07:10.650 --> 01:07:13.880
So live migration is
a feature unique to us

01:07:13.880 --> 01:07:15.270
among other hyperscale clouds.

01:07:15.270 --> 01:07:17.340
We're the only
providers that have it.

01:07:17.340 --> 01:07:20.450
What it allows us to do is to
take a running virtual machine,

01:07:20.450 --> 01:07:24.140
even a virtual machine with
terabytes of local solid state

01:07:24.140 --> 01:07:27.330
disk, doing hundreds of
thousands of operations

01:07:27.330 --> 01:07:30.580
a second to storage, and
move that virtual machine

01:07:30.580 --> 01:07:34.550
to other hosts without
skipping a beat.

01:07:34.550 --> 01:07:37.760
So that means if we're
blowing network alarms,

01:07:37.760 --> 01:07:39.890
or there's a problem
with the storage device,

01:07:39.890 --> 01:07:42.280
or we want to update
the software, patch it

01:07:42.280 --> 01:07:44.550
for security, or we
want to reshuffle

01:07:44.550 --> 01:07:47.140
the layout of infrastructure
to make efficient

01:07:47.140 --> 01:07:50.110
the usage of our
resources, live migration

01:07:50.110 --> 01:07:53.107
allows us to do that
transparently with no downtime.

01:07:53.107 --> 01:07:54.940
You get no email from
us that says, hold on.

01:07:54.940 --> 01:07:57.100
I please need you to
restart your computers.

01:07:57.100 --> 01:07:59.800
That is yesterday's cloud.

01:07:59.800 --> 01:08:02.114
We won't make you do that.

01:08:02.114 --> 01:08:07.190
That allows our services to
not only offer this to you,

01:08:07.190 --> 01:08:09.110
but recognize all
of the tools we've

01:08:09.110 --> 01:08:12.500
built above that infrastructure
inherit that ability.

01:08:12.500 --> 01:08:14.390
They are all more available.

01:08:14.390 --> 01:08:16.740
Our databases, and our
development environments,

01:08:16.740 --> 01:08:18.470
and our processing
infrastructure, they

01:08:18.470 --> 01:08:21.290
all use this capability.

01:08:21.290 --> 01:08:25.930
So your tools can be more highly
available, and so are ours.

01:08:25.930 --> 01:08:28.410
And in order to
have that work, it

01:08:28.410 --> 01:08:32.180
has to stand on top of a totally
unique network infrastructure.

01:08:32.180 --> 01:08:35.439
So we've built a global
software-defined network.

01:08:35.439 --> 01:08:36.689
You can read about this stuff.

01:08:36.689 --> 01:08:39.100
It's in an Andromeda
white paper.

01:08:39.100 --> 01:08:42.430
It's really an incredible
system for orchestrating

01:08:42.430 --> 01:08:46.430
the movement of data, not
just inside of our facilities,

01:08:46.430 --> 01:08:48.510
but across the world.

01:08:48.510 --> 01:08:52.200
In many ways, this allows us
to treat all of our regions

01:08:52.200 --> 01:08:55.800
as a single global
computer, very

01:08:55.800 --> 01:08:57.979
different than a
collection of resources

01:08:57.979 --> 01:09:01.779
you have to figure out
how to connect together.

01:09:01.779 --> 01:09:03.700
And we're growing
that cloud over time.

01:09:03.700 --> 01:09:05.479
So you've already
heard that we're

01:09:05.479 --> 01:09:08.109
very excited to add a
region here in Tokyo.

01:09:08.109 --> 01:09:08.880
I'm excited.

01:09:08.880 --> 01:09:09.859
I hope you're excited.

01:09:09.859 --> 01:09:11.569
It's going to be an
incredible facility.

01:09:11.569 --> 01:09:16.220
But we'll be adding even
more, in fact, 10 more data

01:09:16.220 --> 01:09:20.542
centers over the next year, new
facilities that allow not only

01:09:20.542 --> 01:09:22.500
for you to get better
performance for customers

01:09:22.500 --> 01:09:25.050
locally, but to take
your products to market

01:09:25.050 --> 01:09:27.660
around the world.

01:09:27.660 --> 01:09:30.810
So those additions of new
facilities over time will make

01:09:30.810 --> 01:09:35.479
it so that this system is ready
to serve today's applications

01:09:35.479 --> 01:09:37.340
and tomorrow's.

01:09:37.340 --> 01:09:41.939
So we know that that
connectivity between facilities

01:09:41.939 --> 01:09:44.740
is a core part of our
security story, right?

01:09:44.740 --> 01:09:48.899
This is a global private
Google-owned network.

01:09:48.899 --> 01:09:51.420
This is entirely our
fiber that allows

01:09:51.420 --> 01:09:54.240
us to control not only
access to that network,

01:09:54.240 --> 01:09:57.010
but the routing and performance
of packet transmissions

01:09:57.010 --> 01:09:58.180
across it.

01:09:58.180 --> 01:10:01.390
That ensures that your
applications, even if they're

01:10:01.390 --> 01:10:04.580
located far, far away
from where your users are,

01:10:04.580 --> 01:10:07.640
deliver the very best
performance possible.

01:10:07.640 --> 01:10:09.790
To give you some idea of
the scale of what we're

01:10:09.790 --> 01:10:14.490
talking about, some kind of
view to just how big this is,

01:10:14.490 --> 01:10:17.970
we're looking at a network
today that's over a petabyte

01:10:17.970 --> 01:10:21.940
a second of
bisectional bandwidth.

01:10:21.940 --> 01:10:25.240
For context, that's four
times the size of this thing

01:10:25.240 --> 01:10:29.000
you may have heard of
called the internet.

01:10:29.000 --> 01:10:33.750
So it's [JAPANESE], right?

01:10:33.750 --> 01:10:34.817
So--

01:10:34.817 --> 01:10:36.650
MILES WARD THROUGH
INTERPRETER: It's so big.

01:10:36.650 --> 01:10:39.710
MILES WARD: --when you see these
systems working in concert,

01:10:39.710 --> 01:10:43.050
it allows customers to build
incredible applications.

01:10:43.050 --> 01:10:45.300
I have an example of just
that kind of customer

01:10:45.300 --> 01:10:46.966
that I'd love you to
take a look at now.

